    "report": "The notice of a new message now carries `listingId`. The change is committed as `eb8bdf4` on `main`, the current branch, and not pushed. Typecheck, lint, formatting, the conventions check, all 1049 unit tests and all 607 end-to-end tests pass.\n\n**What changed, from the publisher inward**\n- **Messaging:** `MessageSentEvent` records the conversation's `listingId`, both where a message is sent and where a conversation starts with its first message. `MessageSentHandler` passes it on in the integration event.\n- **Shared kernel:** `MessageSentPayload` gains `listingId`.\n- **Notifications:** the message notice body gains `listingId`. The handler, the owing rule and the body all pass it through. `PostgresNotices` stores it in the `listing_id` column that already existed and reads it back. `HttpNoticeService` sends it with the rest of the body, since it serialises the whole body.\n- **Specs:** the message-body expectations in unit and end-to-end tests now include `listingId`.\n\nWho is told, how often, delivery and retry are unchanged, and the other notice kinds are untouched.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- **Existing unaccepted notices:** a message notice owed before this change and not yet accepted has no stored listing. It is sent with `listingId: null`. The alternative was joining Messaging's `conversations` table from Notifications' schema, which crosses the context boundary, so I didn't. The body type is `string | null` for message notices to make this explicit. If the notification service cannot accept null, those few rows need a backfill decided on separately.\n- **Branch:** the commit went to `main` because you asked for the current branch. The harness default is to branch off the default branch first. If you would rather have a feature branch, it is a quick move.\n\n**Not touched**\n- The clock rule in `CLAUDE.md` says a domain method's time parameter should always be given rather than defaulted. The existing `new Date()` defaults in the message-notice code (`MessageNoticeOwing.owe`, `NoticeWindow.admit`, `Conversation.send`) predate this request, so I left them alone.\n- I did not start a server, so nothing ran on port 4471.",
